From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 12:45:09 +0200
Subject: Re-type literals through a Literate instance in scope
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Squashed from 7 commits, oldest first:

* Literate: re-type literals through user-provided instances (prototype)

A scala.Literate[From] instance in scope grants permission for a literal
of type From to be re-typed as the instance's Result — by convention a
refinement carrying the literal's singleton type, e.g.
Text { type Original = "foo" } — wherever the expected type does not
already accept the literal as it is. String literals are rewritten at
typedLiteral, Int/Double literals at typedNumber's fallback (the
expected-type-driven FromDigits cases take precedence). The rewrite is
speculative with full rollback, and is suppressed in patterns, type
positions, annotations, inline-val rhs, inline expansion, and during
overload-resolution argument pre-typing, where arguments are ranked
against a wildcard. With no instance in scope, behaviour is unchanged.

convert is deliberately not a trait member (a deferred inline method
cannot be invoked through the trait type): instances are named classes
with a concrete inline convert, resolved on the instance's own type, so
the conversion erases to nothing.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude).

* Literate: convert on member selection unconditionally

With an instance in scope, the instance's API is the literal's API: a
selection on a literal always converts, so a partial String method
('foo'.charAt(5)) is a compile error rather than a latent runtime
exception; an ascription (('foo': String).charAt(0)) remains the
explicit way to the underlying API. WildcardSelectionProto — how
singleton types (type Topic = 42, val x: 'foo') type their path — names
no member and keeps the literal.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude).

* Literate: force instance type variables before committing a rewrite

A polymorphic given (given literate: [str <: String & Singleton] =>
TextLiterate[str]) leaves fresh type variables in the speculatively
typed conversion tree; nothing on this re-entrant path interpolates
them, so they escaped into committed trees — surfacing as 'Required:
str' errors in inline method bodies and 'error when pickling type str'
elsewhere. Force both the instance's and the conversion's types with
ForceDegree.all (the argument constrains the variable to the literal's
singleton), and refuse the rewrite if anything remains undefined.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude).

* Literate: exempt synthesized members from literal rewriting

Case-class and enum toString bodies are literals with inferred result
types; converting them broke the Any.toString override. Skip when the
owner is Synthetic, except anonymous functions, whose bodies are user
code.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude).

* Literate: do not rewrite literals in quote patterns; guard against completion cycles

Two fixes found by the Soundness on-state trial.

A literal in a quote pattern is matched structurally against the literal
a macro's caller wrote. Converting it made the pattern expect the
instance's target type, so it silently stopped matching: telekinesis's
'{("", $status: Http.Status)} case fell through to its generic
header case, and every named-argument HTTP response failed to compile
with a misleading message. This is the first defect that changes
behaviour rather than merely rejecting code. Gate on
Mode.isQuotedPattern; a 12-line reproduction is in the spike.

Searching for an instance forces whatever the search touches, including
the root import that supplies it. For a literal in an annotation on a
symbol of the importing unit, that cycles. Declining a rewrite is always
sound, so a TypeError during the search now leaves the literal alone.
Annotation arguments are also suppressed directly in ProtoTypes.typedArg,
which retracts Mode.InAnnotation and so defeated the mode gate.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude).

* Enable literal re-typing with -Z:literate-literals

Without the flag, upstream's code runs: a literal keeps its ordinary type
whether or not a Literate instance is in scope.

* Ship Literate in the supplementary proscala-library jar

The standard library additions of the fork live under library-proscala/src and
are built into their own jar (proscala-library, plus a Scala.js variant), so the
scala-library and scalajs-scalalib jars are byte-identical to what upstream
builds from the same tree. The compiler looks the class up by name and runs
without it.

